<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Why counting tiles beats dividing square footage</h2>
    <p>Dividing floor area by tile area assumes every cut piece gets used somewhere else. In a real room, the last tile in each row is cut to fit, and the offcut is usually too small to use. This calculator counts whole tiles along each wall instead, which is what you will actually lay.</p>
    <p>In a room whose dimensions are close to multiples of the tile size, the 2 methods agree: the default 10 × 8 ft room takes 80 tiles of 12 × 12 in either way. The difference appears in rooms that are not:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Bathroom 9′3″ × 7′3″ (enter 9.25 × 7.25 ft)</th><th class="num">Area method</th><th class="num">Tile count</th><th class="num">With 10% waste</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>12 × 12 in tile</td><td class="num">67 tiles</td><td class="num">80 tiles</td><td class="num">88 tiles</td></tr>
      <tr><td>24 × 24 in tile</td><td class="num">17 tiles</td><td class="num">20 tiles</td><td class="num">22 tiles</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Here the real tile count is about 19% higher than the area method suggests, because each row and column ends in a 3-inch strip that still needs a whole tile. Ordering from the area figure plus 10% waste would leave the job several tiles short.</p>

    <h2>How much waste to allow</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>10%</strong> for a straight grid in a rectangular room.</li>
      <li><strong>15%</strong> for diagonal layouts, herringbone, or rooms with a toilet, tub, vanity, and floor drain to cut around.</li>
      <li><strong>More for large-format tile.</strong> Tiles with an edge of 15 inches or longer leave larger, often unusable offcuts, and each one that breaks costs more.</li>
      <li><strong>More for natural stone</strong> and handmade tile, which vary in color and thickness and need extra for sorting.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Grout joints and layout</h2>
    <p>The grout gap is part of each tile's footprint, which is why the calculator adds it before counting. Rectified tiles, cut to precise dimensions, can use narrower joints; pressed tiles with slight size variation need wider ones. Use the joint width the tile manufacturer recommends.</p>
    <p>Before ordering, dry-lay a row or 2 on the floor. Centering the layout so both sides end with pieces at least half a tile wide looks better than a full tile on one wall and a sliver on the other, and it can change the count.</p>

    <h2>Walls, backsplashes, and showers</h2>
    <p>Measure the surface that will actually be tiled, not the room. A backsplash 30 ft long and 18 in tall is 45 sq ft. Subtract windows and outlets only if they are large; the cuts around them use material anyway. Niches, curbs, and benches each need their own measurement.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Tile questions</h2>
    <details><summary>How many 12 × 12 tiles do I need for 100 square feet?</summary><p>100 tiles cover 100 sq ft exactly; with 10% waste, buy about 110. If the room's dimensions don't divide evenly by 12 inches, count rows and columns instead, which can add more.</p></details>
    <details><summary>How much extra tile should I buy?</summary><p>About 10% for a straight layout, 15% for diagonal or patterned layouts, and more for large-format tile or natural stone. Keep spare tiles for future repairs.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Does grout width change how many tiles I need?</summary><p>Slightly. Wider joints spread tiles farther apart, so fewer tiles fit along a wall. The calculator includes the grout gap when it counts tiles per row.</p></details>
  </section>
